/// Home Assistant's `lock` domain model, vendored.
///
/// A verbatim copy of what HA defines in `homeassistant/components/lock/const.py`
/// — the state vocabulary and the `LockEntityFeature` bit flag — so a dashboard
/// can name a lock state without spelling a magic string, and so the schema's
/// `supportsOpen` has a documented source for the bit it tests.
///
/// Docs: https://developers.home-assistant.io/docs/core/entity/lock
///
/// Safe to vendor for the same reason `hass/light.pkl` is: HA's `*EntityFeature`
/// IntFlags are append-only, never renumbered, because they are persisted in
/// entity state attributes and read by the frontend.
module hass_lock

/// Every state a lock reports. Five of the seven are TRANSIENT — a lock passes
/// through `locking`/`unlocking`/`opening` on its way somewhere, and reports
/// `jammed` when it tried to move and could not.
///
/// [[CANNOT_OPEN]] types against this, and that is the whole reason it lives in
/// this module: the type is what refuses `"unavailable"`, which is not a lock
/// state at all and had been sitting in that list.
///
/// The QUERY layer still does not — it compares a state as a plain String
/// (`q.eq(q.stateProp, "locked")`) — so this remains the vocabulary an author
/// needs rather than a constraint every read enforces. Typing `stateProp` per
/// domain is a query-layer decision, not a lock one.
typealias LockState =
  "locked"|"unlocked"|"locking"|"unlocking"|"open"|"opening"|"jammed"

/// The lock can release its latch — `lock/open`, distinct from unlocking it.
/// The only flag the domain defines.
const OPEN: Int = 1

/// HA's `canOpen`, as the states it says no in: already open, or mid-move.
///
/// `lock/const.py` marks `is_locking`/`is_unlocking`/`is_opening` optional, so a
/// lock that jumps straight `locked`→`unlocked` simply never reports one of
/// these and the control is never inert — which is what HA's own button does
/// there too.
///
/// Typed as [[LockState]], which is the point of it living here rather than
/// beside the card that reads it: the type is what refuses `"unavailable"`.
/// That was in this list, and it does not belong — availability is a different
/// axis, and `tap.inertStates` adds it to every service tap in every domain.
/// The latch's own `lock/open` press is protected by that rule; this list is
/// only about what a LOCK's state means.
const CANNOT_OPEN: List<LockState> = List(
  "open",
  "opening",
  "unlocking",
  "locking"
)
